# Practical: Build a Simple Docker Image to Print “Hello World”

We'll do this **step by step**, starting from installing Docker and ending with running the image.

The final workflow is:

```text
Dockerfile
    ↓
docker build
    ↓
Docker Image
    ↓
docker run
    ↓
Hello World!
```

## Step 1 — Install Docker

Download **Docker Desktop** from the official Docker website:

[Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Install it using the default options.

After installation, **start Docker Desktop**.

Wait until Docker indicates that it is running.

---

# Step 2 — Check Docker installation

Open **Command Prompt / PowerShell / Terminal**.

Run:

```bash
docker --version
```

You should get something similar to:

```text
Docker version 2x.x.x
```

Then run:

```bash
docker run hello-world
```

Docker will download the standard `hello-world` image and run it.

You should see:

```text
Hello from Docker!
This message shows that your installation appears to be working correctly.
```

If you see that message, Docker is working correctly. ✅

> Note: This test uses Docker's existing `hello-world` image. For your **syllabus practical**, we'll create our **own Docker image** next.

---

# Step 3 — Create a project folder

Create a folder called:

```text
docker-hello
```

Windows:

```bash
mkdir docker-hello
cd docker-hello
```

Your folder is currently:

```text
docker-hello/
```

---

# Step 4 — Create a Dockerfile

Inside `docker-hello`, create a file named exactly:

```text
Dockerfile
```

**Important:** It should be `Dockerfile`, not `Dockerfile.txt`.

Put this inside:

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

CMD ["echo", "Hello World"]
```

Save the file.

Your project now looks like:

```text
docker-hello/
└── Dockerfile
```

---

# Step 5 — Understand the Dockerfile

### `FROM`

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest
```

This tells Docker to use the lightweight **Alpine Linux** image as the base image.

### `CMD`

```dockerfile
CMD ["echo", "Hello World"]
```

This specifies the command that will execute when the container starts.

So:

```text
Container starts
      ↓
echo "Hello World"
      ↓
Hello World
```

---

# Step 6 — Build the Docker image

Make sure your terminal is inside the `docker-hello` directory.

Run:

```bash
docker build -t hello-world-image .
```

### What does this mean?

```text
docker build
```

Build a Docker image.

```text
-t hello-world-image
```

Give the image the name:

```text
hello-world-image
```

```text
.
```

Use the current directory as the build context.

You should eventually see something similar to:

```text
Successfully built ...
Successfully tagged hello-world-image:latest
```

Depending on your Docker version, the output format may look slightly different.

---

# Step 7 — Check the image

Run:

```bash
docker images
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
REPOSITORY          TAG       IMAGE ID       SIZE
hello-world-image   latest    xxxxxxxx       ...
```

Your custom Docker image has been created. ✅

---

# Step 8 — Run the Docker container

Now run:

```bash
docker run hello-world-image
```

Expected output:

```text
Hello World
```

🎉 You have successfully built and executed your own Docker image.

---

# Step 9 — Check the container

Run:

```bash
docker ps -a
```

You should see your container listed.

For example:

```text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE              STATUS
xxxxxxxx       hello-world-image  Exited (0)
```

Why does it say **Exited**?

Because our program only executes:

```bash
echo "Hello World"
```

Once `echo` finishes, there is nothing else for the container to do, so it exits.

That is completely normal for this practical.

---

# Step 10 — Run the image again

You can run it again whenever you want:

```bash
docker run hello-world-image
```

Output:

```text
Hello World
```

Each `docker run` creates a new container from the image.

---

# Step 11 — See all your containers

Run:

```bash
docker ps -a
```

You may see multiple containers:

```text
hello-world-image
hello-world-image
hello-world-image
```

because you ran the image multiple times.

---

# Step 12 — Remove stopped containers

If you want to clean them up, you can remove a container using its ID:

```bash
docker rm CONTAINER_ID
```

For example:

```bash
docker rm abc123
```

You can also remove unused stopped containers with:

```bash
docker container prune
```

Docker will ask for confirmation.

---

# Step 13 — Remove the image

When you're completely finished with the practical, you can remove your image:

```bash
docker rmi hello-world-image
```

Check:

```bash
docker images
```

The image should no longer appear.

**Don't run this step until you've finished testing the image.**

